{"id":"f8cb1e93-5c47-4338-a25f-01847a604555","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885076,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    refinement = case[\\\"refinement\\\"]\\n    edges = case[\\\"edges\\\"]\\n    community_of_node = {}\\n    for node, comm in refinement.items():\\n        community_of_node[node] = comm\\n    members = {}\\n    for node, comm in community_of_node.items():\\n        members.setdefault(comm, []).append(node)\\n    for comm in members:\\n        members[comm].sort()\\n    agg = {}\\n    for src, dst, w in edges:\\n        c1 = community_of_node[src]\\n        c2 = community_of_node[dst]\\n        key = tuple(sorted((c1, c2)))\\n        agg[key] = agg.get(key, 0) + w\\n    aggregate_edges = []\\n    for (c1, c2), w in agg.items():\\n        aggregate_edges.append([c1, c2, w])\\n    aggregate_edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\\n    return {\\\"aggregate_edges\\\": aggregate_edges, \\\"members\\\": members}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem: task DATA-LEI-02. The output must be a JSON object with keys \"aggregate_edges\" and \"members\" following the schema. The schema says:\n- \"aggregate_edges\": array of arrays of length 3: [string, string, number].\n- \"members\": object with dynamic keys from \"case.refinement.values()\" (i.e., the values of the refinement dictionary). Each value is an array of strings.\n\nSo, given a case that has an \"edges\" list and a \"refinement\" dictionary (mapping node string to community label string), we need to produce the aggregated graph after refinement.\n\nThe description: \"Leiden refinement and aggregation phases on registered miniature graphs; stochastic node order is replaced by a frozen permutation\". But the case does not provide a permutation; we have to assume the node order is irrelevant? Actually, the task seems to just aggregate edges according to refinement. It says \"Map each original node to exactly one refined community.\" That is given by refinement. \"Map every edge endpoint through that refinement.\" Then \"Canonicalize each aggregate endpoint pair so undirected duplicates coincide.\" Means if we have edge (u,v) with weight w, and refinement maps u->A, v->B, then we add an edge (A,B) with weight w. Since it's undirected, (A,B) and (B,A) are the same; we should treat them as one edge. \"Sum all weights for each aggregate pair including self-loops.\" So if two nodes map to same community, that's a self-loop at that community level; weight contributed.\n\n\"Return sorted aggregate edges and sorted membership lists.\" For aggregate_edges: sorted? The schema doesn't specify order, but we can sort for determinism. Probably by first then second node string. For members: the dynamic keys are the refinement values (unique community labels). The value should be the list of nodes that map to that community, sorted.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Parse case: case has \"edges\", \"refinement\". Possibly also \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n- Build mapping from node to community: the refinement dict.\n- Determine communities: set of values from refinement.\n- Build a dict aggregate_edges_dict: key = (comm1, comm2) sorted tuple (to canonicalize undirected) and value = sum of weights.\n- For each edge in edges: each edge is [src, dst, weight]. Map src->c1, dst->c2. The pair (c1, c2) canonicalize: sorted tuple (min, max). Add weight to that key.\n- Also, if src==dst (self-loop) but still map to same community, it becomes a self-loop at community level.\n- After processing all edges, build list of aggregate_edges: each entry [comm1, comm2, total_weight]. Since we canonicalized, comm1 <= comm2. Then sort by (comm1, comm2) (lexicographically). This matches \"sorted aggregate edges\".\n- For members: get unique communities. For each community, list nodes that map to it, sort alphabetically (or as given? likely strings, sort naturally). Then members object: key is community string, value is sorted list of node strings.\n- Return {\"aggregate_edges\": sorted_edges, \"members\": members_dict}.\n\nNote: The schema says \"dynamicKeysFrom\": \"case.refinement.values()\" meaning the keys of members object must be exactly the values of refinement, but not necessarily all? Actually it says \"dynamicKeysFrom means the object keys are arbitrary JSON strings that must be copied verbatim from the indicated input location\". So the keys must be the exact strings that appear as values in refinement. They may not be unique? In refinement dict, values can repeat. So we should take the set of values. But the schema says \"dynamicKeysFrom: case.refinement.values()\" - values() returns all values, possibly duplicates. So we need to ensure keys are present for each unique value. That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: refinement may have nodes not appearing in edges? But we still need to include them in members if they have no edges? Possibly. The members should include all nodes that are mapped? Since refinement maps original nodes, yes. So for each node in refinement keys, we add to its community's list.\n\nAlso, if an edge endpoint maps to a community but that node might not be in refinement? But refinement should cover all nodes that appear in edges. Assume input is consistent.\n\nImplementation in solve(case):\n- Input case is dict with \"edges\", \"refinement\".\n- Use standard library only (json, etc. not needed for processing, just return dict).\n- Ensure function returns a dict with the two keys. Output must be JSON-serializable (Python dict/list/str/float/int).\n\nWe need to provide Python source code as a string inside JSON. The entire response must be one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we'll output like:\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case): ...\"}\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes properly.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The schema says \"aggregate_edges\" items must be arrays of length 3: [string, string, number]. The number can be int or float? In input edges weights are integers, so sum will be integer. Use int.\n\nAlso, for members, the schema says each value is array of strings.\n\nWe'll sort edges by first then second string.\n\nWe'll sort node lists by string order.\n\nNow, let's craft the solve function.\n\nPseudo:\ndef solve(case):\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    # Build community sets\n    community_map = {}  # node -> community\n    for node, comm in refinement.items():\n        community_map[node] = comm\n    # Invert map: community -> list of nodes\n    members_dict = {}\n    for node, comm in community_map.items():\n        members_dict.setdefault(comm, []).append(node)\n    # Sort each list\n    for comm in members_dict:\n        members_dict[comm].sort()\n    # Aggregate edges\n    aggregate = {}  # key (c1, c2) sorted tuple -> weight sum\n    for src, dst, w in edges:\n        c1 = community_map[src]\n        c2 = community_map[dst]\n        key = tuple(sorted((c1, c2)))  # (min, max)\n        aggregate[key] = aggregate.get(key, 0) + w\n    # Build sorted list\n    aggregate_edges = []\n    for (c1, c2), weight in aggregate.items():\n        aggregate_edges.append([c1, c2, weight])\n    aggregate_edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges, \"members\": members_dict}\n\nCheck: members object keys must be all refinement values; we have exactly that because we iterate over all nodes. If a community has no nodes? Not possible because refinement maps each node to some community. So all communities appear.\n\nNow produce final JSON.\n\nNote: The output must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value must be a string containing the Python source.\n\nWe'll write the source as a raw string, then escape.\n\nWe need to ensure no extra spaces or newlines in the JSON except within the string.\n\nLet's create the solution."},"logprobs":null,"f